# cloudtasks-queue.yaml.example - Cloud Tasks queue pattern for atomic-agents
#
# NOT YET DOGFOODED against the live GCP platform.
# Claims verified against GCP documentation as of 2026-06-17.
# See extras/gcp/README.md §External-claim verification status.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS:
# This file shows gcloud CLI commands to create a Cloud Tasks queue and
# illustrates how Cloud Tasks tasks are created with the correct Idempotency-Key
# header. It is NOT a declarative Terraform/API resource file.
#
# WHY CLOUD TASKS IS SIMPLER THAN PUB/SUB FOR INGESTION:
# Cloud Tasks HTTP targets control the full request (URL, headers, body) at
# task-creation time. This means NO transform Cloud Function is needed. The
# operator sets the Idempotency-Key header directly in the task definition.
# The framework reads the Idempotency-Key header — NOT the X-CloudTasks-TaskName
# header — so the operator must set it explicitly.
#
# ─── IDEMPOTENCY KEY — CRITICAL: DO NOT USE X-CloudTasks-TaskName DIRECTLY ───
#
# Cloud Tasks injects X-CloudTasks-TaskName on push requests to Cloud Run, but
# the value IS the SHORT task name (the task ID segment), NOT the full resource
# path. Example: if the task resource name is:
#   projects/myproject/locations/us-central1/queues/myqueue/tasks/my-task-001
# then X-CloudTasks-TaskName = "my-task-001"
#
# (Verified: X-CloudTasks-TaskName is "the short name of the task" at
#  https://cloud.google.com/tasks/docs/creating-http-target-tasks)
#
# However, even if you set Idempotency-Key from X-CloudTasks-TaskName inside
# a transform, the serve-layer Idempotency-Key validation (in serve/_app.py, a
# strict SUPERSET of the backend's _validate_key in idempotency/filesystem.py;
# it additionally rejects backslash as cross-platform defense) rejects any
# Idempotency-Key value containing "/" with HTTP 422. The short name
# (X-CloudTasks-TaskName) has NO slashes, so it is safe. The full resource path
# would be rejected.
#
# CORRECT APPROACH: Set Idempotency-Key explicitly in the task's httpRequest.headers
# at task-creation time. Use the bare task ID (no slashes):
#
#   httpRequest.headers["Idempotency-Key"] = "my-task-001"
#
# Do NOT rely on X-CloudTasks-TaskName as the dedup key. Two reasons, NEITHER of
# which is a slash/422 problem (X-CloudTasks-TaskName is the SHORT name with no
# slashes — see above):
#   (1) The serve layer reads ONLY the configured Idempotency-Key header (default
#       Idempotency-Key — see the §WHY CLOUD TASKS IS SIMPLER note above), not
#       X-CloudTasks-TaskName — so the header must be set explicitly at task
#       creation regardless.
#   (2) The short name is unique only within a project+queue — see §CROSS-QUEUE
#       UNIQUENESS below; multi-queue deployments would false-dedup.
# (The full RESOURCE PATH does contain slashes and WOULD be rejected with HTTP 422,
#  but you never inject that path as the header — so 422 is not the reason here.)
#
# ─── CROSS-QUEUE UNIQUENESS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# X-CloudTasks-TaskName is the short name, unique within a project+queue.
# Two tasks in DIFFERENT queues MAY share the same short name — they are not
# globally unique. If you have multiple queues routing to the same agent, the
# Idempotency-Key must incorporate the queue name to avoid false dedup:
#
#   Idempotency-Key: "{queue-name}-{task-id}"   # e.g. "ingestion-my-task-001"
#
# For single-queue-per-agent deployments (the natural v0 shape), the bare task
# ID is sufficient. Document this assumption explicitly for your deployment.
# X-CloudTasks-QueueName is also injected and can be concatenated at transform time.
#
# ─── CLOUD TASKS DEDUP WINDOW ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Cloud Tasks enforces a name-based dedup window (best-effort): after a task with
# a given name is successfully deleted or completed, a new task with the same name
# cannot be created for a period.
# DOC-LITERAL FIGURE: the Cloud Tasks overview states "Cloud Tasks remembers task
#  names for up to 24 hours after the task has been deleted from the queue"
#  (https://cloud.google.com/tasks/docs/dual-overview). The REST `name`-field
#  reference gives NO figure (it only constrains the name format), so treat 24h as
#  the doc-of-record window.
# UNVERIFIED — the previously-cited ~1-hour (Cloud-Tasks-created queues) and
#  ~9-day (legacy queue.yaml/App Engine queues) per-queue-type figures could NOT
#  be located in the cited docs as of 2026-06-17 (the overview gives a single
#  uniform "up to 24 hours"). Do NOT rely on a queue-type-specific window; treat
#  the dedup window as best-effort and bounded by the doc-literal "up to 24 hours,"
#  and lean on the agent-side ledger (below) for durable dedup.
#
# This dedup is at the Cloud Tasks level (task creation), NOT at the agent level.
# The agent-side FilesystemDedupLedger is the DURABLE dedup layer (spec/45).
# After the Cloud Tasks dedup window expires, a task with the same name CAN be
# re-created, and the agent-side ledger still provides dedup until the ledger
# entry expires or the container is replaced.
#
# Important: Cloud Tasks dedup by name is BEST-EFFORT and bounded (doc-literal:
# "up to 24 hours" per the overview) — it is NOT an indefinite global uniqueness
# guarantee. Rely on the agent-side ledger for durable dedup.
#
# ─── OIDC AUTH FOR PRIVATE CLOUD RUN ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Cloud Tasks HTTP tasks hitting a private Cloud Run service need OIDC auth.
# The task's httpRequest.oidcToken must specify the service account email and
# audience (the Cloud Run service URL, no trailing slash).
#
# IAM grant required (same pattern as Cloud Scheduler):
#   gcloud run services add-iam-policy-binding <your-agent-name> \
#     --member=serviceAccount:TASKS_SA@PROJECT.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
#     --role=roles/run.invoker \
#     --region=REGION
#
# ─── SINGLE-HOST DEDUP CONSTRAINT ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# FilesystemDedupLedger uses O_EXCL atomicity (single-host only; spec/45
# MUST 7 single_host_only=True). The Cloud Run service MUST remain at
# max-instances=1 (see cloudrun-service.yaml). Two instances do not share a
# filesystem idempotency/ directory — each would see every key as FRESH,
# defeating dedup.
#
# ─── CONCURRENCY: maxConcurrentDispatches ────────────────────────────────────
#
# Cloud Tasks queues default to maxConcurrentDispatches=1000. On a Cloud Run
# service with containerConcurrency=1 and max-instances=1 (the v0 default),
# excess concurrent dispatches queue inside Cloud Run. The FilesystemLockBackend
# holds thread-pool workers for up to 30s — enough concurrent same-agent Cloud
# Tasks dispatches can saturate the pool (TENSIONS.md T2). Recommended:
# set maxConcurrentDispatches=1 to serialize dispatches at the queue level.
# (Verified field name: retryConfig does not control concurrency; the field is
# maxConcurrentDispatches at the queue level —
# https://cloud.google.com/tasks/docs/reference/rest/v2/projects.locations.queues)
#
# ─── X-CloudTasks-* INJECTED HEADERS ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Cloud Tasks injects these headers on HTTP-target task delivery to Cloud Run
# (verified at https://cloud.google.com/tasks/docs/creating-http-target-tasks).
#
# ALWAYS injected:
#   X-CloudTasks-QueueName          — short queue name
#   X-CloudTasks-TaskName           — short task name (last path segment)
#   X-CloudTasks-TaskRetryCount     — number of retries so far
#   X-CloudTasks-TaskExecutionCount — number of executions so far
#   X-CloudTasks-TaskETA            — task ETA in seconds since epoch
#
# CONDITIONALLY present ("additional headers that might be present" per the doc —
# do NOT assume they exist; e.g. TaskPreviousResponse is ABSENT on the first
# attempt, so reading it unconditionally raises KeyError):
#   X-CloudTasks-TaskPreviousResponse — HTTP response code from previous attempt
#   X-CloudTasks-TaskRetryReason      — why the task is being retried
#
# These headers are READ-ONLY on the serve side (the framework doesn't read them
# — only the Idempotency-Key header is read for dedup). They are useful for
# logging/debugging inside the agent's work_item processing.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── HOW TO RUN THESE COMMANDS ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every command below is commented out (each line begins with `#`). To run one,
# remove the leading `#` from the command lines — AND delete the interspersed
# annotation lines (the `# Number of delivery attempts...`-style explanations
# that sit between a flag's trailing `\` and the next flag). Those annotation
# lines are documentation, NOT part of the command: left in place after
# uncommenting, a comment line between two `\`-continued lines breaks the
# backslash continuation in most shells. Uncomment the flag lines only.
#
# ── STEP 0: Set shared variables ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# PROJECT_ID=your-project-id
# LOCATION=us-central1
# QUEUE_NAME=atomic-agents-queue
# AGENT_NAME=your-agent-name
# TASKS_SA=cloudtasks-sa@${PROJECT_ID}.iam.gserviceaccount.com
# CLOUD_RUN_URL=https://YOUR_CLOUD_RUN_SERVICE_URL   # no trailing slash

# ── STEP 1: Create the Cloud Tasks queue ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# Field names verified against the Cloud Tasks Queue REST API resource at
# https://cloud.google.com/tasks/docs/reference/rest/v2/projects.locations.queues
# (RetryConfig schema):
#   retryConfig.maxAttempts:      integer (-1 for unlimited)
#   retryConfig.maxRetryDuration: duration string e.g. "3600s"
#   retryConfig.minBackoff:       duration string e.g. "10s"
#   retryConfig.maxBackoff:       duration string e.g. "300s"
#   retryConfig.maxDoublings:     integer
#
# gcloud CLI flag names differ from REST API field names:
#   --max-attempts            → retryConfig.maxAttempts
#   --max-retry-duration      → retryConfig.maxRetryDuration
#   --min-backoff             → retryConfig.minBackoff
#   --max-backoff             → retryConfig.maxBackoff
#   --max-doublings           → retryConfig.maxDoublings
#   --max-concurrent-dispatches → maxConcurrentDispatches (queue-level, not retryConfig)

# gcloud tasks queues create ${QUEUE_NAME} \
#   --location=${LOCATION} \
#   --project=${PROJECT_ID} \
#   \
#   --max-attempts=5 \
#   # Number of delivery attempts before the task fails permanently.
#   # IMPORTANT: Cloud Tasks has NO built-in dead-letter queue. After
#   # max-attempts (or max-retry-duration), the task is DROPPED — it is not
#   # captured anywhere. (--log-sampling-ratio is unrelated: it controls the
#   # FRACTION [0.0-1.0] of queue operations written to Cloud Logging, not
#   # dead-lettering. Verified: gcloud tasks queues create reference.)
#   # To capture terminal failures, the WORKER must detect exhaustion — e.g.
#   # inspect X-CloudTasks-TaskRetryCount approaching max-attempts on the final
#   # attempt — and persist/re-enqueue the work item itself; OR route through
#   # Pub/Sub instead (which DOES have a native deadLetterPolicy). This matches
#   # the README §When to use comparison table ("Dead-letter queue: Not built-in").
#   #
#   # REDELIVERY COST + NO TERMINAL ARTIFACT (the asymmetry vs Pub/Sub): Cloud
#   # Tasks retries EVERY non-2xx serve response (402 cost-skip, 409 in-flight,
#   # 422 bad-body, 503 lock-busy) up to max-attempts, re-spending any mid-loop
#   # partial LLM budget on each attempt — exactly as the Pub/Sub 402 path warns,
#   # but there is NO transform to ACK-route a deterministic 4xx away. And because
#   # there is no DLQ, a task exhausted on a deterministic 402/422 is silently
#   # DROPPED (work LOST) with NO terminal GCP-side artifact to inspect — only the
#   # N per-attempt JSONL records (skipped/error). Correlate them by
#   # Idempotency-Key (each redelivery shares the bare task ID) and treat the
#   # attempt at X-CloudTasks-TaskRetryCount == max-attempts-1 as the terminal
#   # drop. 503 (lock_busy) is the one TRANSIENT non-2xx — retry succeeds, but a
#   # persistent 503 storm means lock-pool saturation (lower max-concurrent-
#   # dispatches), not a poison task. For inspectable terminal failures, prefer
#   # the Pub/Sub deadLetterPolicy path. See README §Redelivery behavior.
#   \
#   --max-retry-duration=3600s \
#   # retryConfig.maxRetryDuration: total time Cloud Tasks keeps retrying.
#   # Duration string required (e.g. "3600s", "1h" may not be accepted — use seconds).
#   \
#   --min-backoff=10s \
#   # retryConfig.minBackoff: minimum wait between retries.
#   \
#   --max-backoff=300s \
#   # retryConfig.maxBackoff: maximum wait between retries (caps exponential growth).
#   \
#   --max-doublings=4 \
#   # retryConfig.maxDoublings: number of times backoff doubles before max-backoff is applied.
#   \
#   --max-concurrent-dispatches=1
#   # Limit concurrent dispatches to 1 for Cloud Run containerConcurrency=1.
#   # See §CONCURRENCY above. Increase only after verifying the serving topology.

# ── STEP 2: Apply IAM grant ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# gcloud run services add-iam-policy-binding ${AGENT_NAME} \
#   --member=serviceAccount:${TASKS_SA} \
#   --role=roles/run.invoker \
#   --region=${LOCATION}

# ── STEP 3: Create a task (example — replace with your task creation logic) ───
#
# CRITICAL: Set Idempotency-Key in httpRequest.headers to the BARE TASK ID.
# Do NOT pass the full resource path (contains slashes → HTTP 422 from serve).
#
# Using gcloud (for illustration — production code typically uses the Cloud Tasks
# Python client library):
#
# TASK_ID=my-task-$(date +%s%N)   # bare alphanumeric ID, no slashes
# WORK_ITEM_JSON='{"work_item": "Process batch 42"}'
#
# NOTE on gcloud flags (verified against the gcloud tasks create-http-task
# reference, https://docs.cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/tasks/create-http-task):
#   - TASK_ID is a POSITIONAL argument, NOT a flag. There is no --task-name flag.
#     Passing the task ID positionally is what ENABLES Cloud Tasks de-duplication
#     (the best-effort name-dedup window; see §CLOUD TASKS DEDUP WINDOW above);
#     omit it and Cloud Tasks auto-generates a random name and the dedup window
#     does NOT apply.
#   - The body flag is --body-content (or --body-file). There is no --body flag.
#
# gcloud tasks create-http-task ${TASK_ID} \
#   --queue=${QUEUE_NAME} \
#   --location=${LOCATION} \
#   --project=${PROJECT_ID} \
#   --url="${CLOUD_RUN_URL}/agents/${AGENT_NAME}/call" \
#   --method=POST \
#   --header="Content-Type: application/json" \
#   --header="Idempotency-Key: ${TASK_ID}" \
#   # Idempotency-Key = bare task ID (no slashes; same value as the positional TASK_ID).
#   # Cloud Tasks dedup window for this name: best-effort, doc-literal "up to
#   #   24 hours" (see §CLOUD TASKS DEDUP WINDOW above).
#   # For single-queue deployments: task ID is unique enough.
#   # For multi-queue deployments: prefix with queue name:
#   #   --header="Idempotency-Key: ${QUEUE_NAME}-${TASK_ID}"
#   --body-content="${WORK_ITEM_JSON}" \
#   --oidc-service-account-email="${TASKS_SA}" \
#   --oidc-token-audience="${CLOUD_RUN_URL}"
#
# Python client library equivalent (preferred for production):
#
#   import json
#   from google.cloud import tasks_v2
#   client = tasks_v2.CloudTasksClient()
#   parent = client.queue_path(PROJECT_ID, LOCATION, QUEUE_NAME)
#   task_id = "my-task-001"  # bare alphanumeric ID — no slashes
#   task = {
#       "name": f"{parent}/tasks/{task_id}",
#       "http_request": {
#           "http_method": tasks_v2.HttpMethod.POST,
#           "url": f"{CLOUD_RUN_URL}/agents/{AGENT_NAME}/call",
#           "headers": {
#               "Content-Type": "application/json",
#               # Idempotency-Key = bare task ID (no slashes).
#               # The framework reads this header for dedup (spec/45).
#               # It does NOT read X-CloudTasks-TaskName automatically.
#               "Idempotency-Key": task_id,
#           },
#           "body": json.dumps({"work_item": "Process batch 42"}).encode(),
#           "oidc_token": {
#               "service_account_email": TASKS_SA,
#               "audience": CLOUD_RUN_URL,  # no trailing slash
#           },
#       },
#   }
#   client.create_task(request={"parent": parent, "task": task})

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# See extras/gcp/README.md §Cloud Tasks ingestion runbook for the full operator
# guide, idempotency contract, and comparison with the Pub/Sub path.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
